# Citation metadata. GitHub renders a "Cite this repository" button from this
# file, and Zenodo reads it when minting a DOI for a release.
#
# Authors, title and keywords are taken from the manuscript
# (section/whole-article.tex). ORCIDs are the one field that cannot be
# recovered from the draft -- add them before the first release, because
# Zenodo matches contributors on ORCID.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Controversy and Group Certainty Jointly Shape Everyday Moral Judgments: code and data"
message: "If you use this code or data, please cite both the paper and this repository."
type: software

authors:
  - given-names: Ziyu
    family-names: Chen
    email: Ziyu.Chen@anu.edu.au
    affiliation: The Australian National University, Australia
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
  - given-names: Lexing
    family-names: Xie
    affiliation: The Australian National University, Australia
  - given-names: Minjeong
    family-names: Shin
    affiliation: The Australian National University, Australia
  - given-names: Tuan Dung
    family-names: Nguyen
    affiliation: University of Pennsylvania, United States
  - given-names: Colin
    family-names: Klein
    affiliation: The Australian National University, Australia
  - given-names: Chenhao
    family-names: Tan
    affiliation: University of Chicago, United States
  - given-names: Nick
    family-names: Schuster
    affiliation: University of Georgia, United States
  - given-names: Nicholas George
    family-names: Carroll
    affiliation: The Australian National University, Australia
  - given-names: Alasdair
    family-names: Tran
    affiliation: The Australian National University, Australia

repository-code: "https://github.com/ZiyuChen0410/Controversy-and-Group-Certainty"

abstract: >-
  Code and public data reproducing the figures and statistics for a study of
  how people revise moral judgments after seeing how others judged the same
  real-life dilemma. Participants judged Reddit "Am I the Asshole?" dilemmas,
  were shown one of four signal conditions describing the controversy and
  group certainty of others' judgments, then judged again. The analyses
  characterise when people weakened their position (changed verdict, or held
  it with less confidence) and when they strengthened it.

keywords:
  - moral judgment
  - social influence
  - opinion change
  - aggregate opinion
  - controversy
  - group certainty
  - minority influence

license: MIT
license-url: "https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT"

# `license` above refers to the code. The data in survey_data/ is released
# under CC BY 4.0, and the restricted tier is not openly licensed -- see
# README.md.

